  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1025 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nOur next trough is currently located over Southern Canadian \nPlains. This trough slides east into the Great Lakes region by \nMonday afternoon. In the meantime, a quiet night is anticipated \nwith temperatures falling into the mid 30s to low 40s. As the \naforementioned trough moves into the Great Lakes, a cold front \nsags southeast into the area. Ahead of the front, southwest \nwinds and sunny skies help to produce a very mild afternoon. \nWidespread 60s and even a few 70s are likely. Other than passing\nclouds, this initial front is forecast to remain dry. Even \nthough surface high pressure settles into the region by Tuesday \nafternoon, only a minor temperature decrease is expected.\n\nThe next trough quickly digs southeast into the Central Plains \nand phases with another trough over the Great Lakes. With \nlimited moisture to work with, this next system will be largely \ndry, though a low chance of showers over western Iowa remains \npossible Wednesday morning. Cooler temperatures then fill in \nbehind this second cold front associated with this system. High\ntemperatures begin to fall into the 50s on Wednesday with highs\nin the 40s returning Thursday. At the same time, low \ntemperatures fall into the 30s. By Friday morning, a strong \nsurface high pressure settles into the area. Calm winds and \nclear skies are poised to help temperatures fall below freezing \nacross the area Friday morning. Widespread upper 20s and low \n30s appears likely for all.\n\nBy next weekend, temperatures begin to climb again as upper-\nlevel ridging builds over the Central Plains. High temperatures\nin the 50s return on Saturday and 60s by Sunday. It remains dry\nthrough next weekend with our next shot of rain not appearing \nuntil sometime during the following week.",
